// A USER input field literally named `__cedar_flag` (declared in LoginInput).
// It nests to `input.__cedar_flag`, distinct from the reserved `__cedar_*`
// slots which live at the top level — so a name beginning with `__cedar_`
// is NOT automatically "reserved"; reserved-ness is a matter of where the
// schema places the field, not a prefix test on its name.
//
// A Read is permitted only if the SAME user formerly logged in with
// `input.__cedar_flag == "on"`, matched by a LITERAL. The interpreter
// resolves `input.__cedar_flag` by descending the `input` group; it has no
// prefix rule that would drop or specially treat the field. A differential
// temporal engine that skips any `__cedar_`-prefixed arg (treating it as a
// reserved positional slot) would fail to serialize / read this field.
//
// Uses the DEFAULT event schema (no per-case override): the field is an
// ordinary spliced input field that merely happens to be named oddly.
//
// Controls:
//   trace_1: past Login with input.__cedar_flag "on" (matches) -> PERMITTED.
//   trace_2: past Login with input.__cedar_flag "off" (does not match) -> NOT
//            permitted (the literal genuinely constrains the match).
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user, input.__cedar_flag: "on" }
};
